---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="The stack" description="How Home Assistant, Predbat, Sigenergy, Intelligent Octopus Go and Solcast run a UK solar and battery system automatically, including the pipeline that publishes this site's stats.">
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>The brain</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Home Assistant on a <strong>£50 second-hand Dell OptiPlex</strong>, with Predbat re-planning
      every half hour against half-hourly prices and a Solcast forecast. Four layers, top to bottom.
      The software didn't cost a penny.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card steps">
    <div class="step">
      <span class="n">01</span>
      <div>
        <h3>Read everything</h3>
        <p>The inverter speaks Modbus TCP over the local network. Every meter, cell voltage and
        MPPT string lands in Home Assistant — over 250 entities, all local — and nothing leaves the
        house except the nightly summary that feeds this site.</p>
        <p class="parts">Home Assistant OS · Sigenergy ESS integration · SQLite recorder</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step">
      <span class="n">02</span>
      <div>
        <h3>Forecast the day</h3>
        <p>Solcast gives two forecasts a day for each roof plane, east and west modelled
        separately. Octopus publishes tomorrow's rates in the afternoon, so by teatime the planner
        knows roughly what both sides of the meter will do.</p>
        <p class="parts">Solcast · Octopus Energy integration · half-hourly rates</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step">
      <span class="n">03</span>
      <div>
        <h3>Make a plan</h3>
        <p>Predbat optimises the next 48 hours: charge windows, discharge windows, and the slots
        where doing nothing is cheapest. It writes the plan straight to the inverter through three
        small bridge automations that map its requested mode onto Sigenergy's EMS registers.</p>
        <p class="parts">Predbat via AppDaemon · 48h horizon · 30-minute slots</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step">
      <span class="n">04</span>
      <div>
        <h3>Publish it</h3>
        <p>At 00:15 an automation rolls up the day, compares it with Predbat's no-solar baseline,
        and pushes one JSON file to this site's GitHub repo. A GitHub Action rebuilds and deploys.
        No editing step, which is rather the point.</p>
        <p class="parts">rest_command → GitHub Contents API · Actions · static build</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="card dark" style="margin-top:1rem">
    <div class="card-head"><h2>What a plan actually looks like</h2></div>
    <p style="max-width:42rem">
      Every half hour Predbat picks one of three moves for each upcoming slot. Overnight it buys
      cheap, at dusk it holds, and mid-morning it decides whether the forecast justifies leaving
      the battery empty for the sun to fill.
    </p>
    <div class="cols-3" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <div class="subcard">
        <p class="kicker" style="color:var(--accent-pale)">Charge</p>
        <p style="margin:0">Buy in the cheapest slots, but only as much as tomorrow's forecast says
        we'll need — never so much that we waste capacity the sun would have filled.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="subcard">
        <p class="kicker" style="color:var(--accent-pale)">Hold</p>
        <p style="margin:0">Sit still through mid-price slots. Doing nothing is very often the
        cheapest available move, and the one people forget to model.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="subcard">
        <p class="kicker" style="color:var(--accent-pale)">Discharge</p>
        <p style="margin:0">Carry the house through the 16:00–19:00 peak, and discharge to the grid
        when Axle calls an event at £1/kWh.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="note">
    <p><strong>The one rule that explains everything:</strong> the export rate is a flat 12p all
    day, so there are no export peaks to chase. The battery is there to feed the house, not the
    grid. For each kWh the order of value goes: avoid evening peak import, then grab the £1/kWh VPP
    events, then avoid standard-rate import, and only export at 12p as a last resort. Predbat
    works all of this out from the rates on its own. None of it is hard-coded.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section-head"><h2>The pieces</h2></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What</th><th>Job</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>OS</td><td>Home Assistant OS (x86-64)</td><td>Runs on the OptiPlex's SSD, which the statistics database likes a lot more than a Pi's SD card</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Inverter link</td><td>Sigenergy ESS integration (HACS)</td><td>Modbus TCP on port 502. Over 250 entities, all local, no cloud</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Tariff</td><td>Octopus Energy integration (HACS)</td><td>Live half-hourly import and export rates, Saving Sessions, consumption</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Forecast</td><td>Solcast (HACS)</td><td>7-day rooftop PV forecast, both roof faces, free hobbyist tier</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Brain</td><td><strong>Predbat</strong> (add-on mode)</td><td>Plans charge and discharge against prices, forecast and learned consumption</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Bridge</td><td>Three small HA automations</td><td>Map Predbat's requested mode onto Sigenergy's EMS controls. <a href="/configs/">See configs</a></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="section-head"><h2>The extras</h2></div>

  <h3>Octopus Saving Sessions</h3>
  <p>The Octopus integration joins every session automatically. Predbat knows about them too: it
  tops the battery up beforehand, then force-discharges through the session window. A one-hour
  session usually shifts about 7 kWh, and the Octopoints come out at £3 to £4 per kWh against the
  10-day baseline. Sessions only run November to March, so they contribute nothing to the numbers
  on this site yet.</p>

  <h3><img src="/axle-logo-black.png" alt="Axle" class="axle-logo-inline" /> VPP, events-only</h3>
  <p>Signed up in June 2026 in <strong>events-only</strong> mode. Axle dispatches the battery four
  to eight times a month during grid stress and pays <strong>£1/kWh</strong>, with a £10/month
  minimum topped up if events are thin. Predbat has Axle support built in, so it pulls upcoming
  events straight into its plan, pre-charges for them, and steps aside cleanly while one runs. The
  rest of the time Predbat stays in charge. Because Axle settles through the smart meter directly
  it paid out for months before our Octopus export was certified, which made it our first real
  export earner. If an Axle event lands inside a Saving Session you earn from both — the two
  schemes have no idea about each other.</p>

  <h3>Load shifting</h3>
  <p>Tapo P110 smart plugs on the washing machine, dryer and dishwasher, plus a phone notification
  when solar surplus goes over 2 kW. Low-tech, and worth about £65 a year.</p>

  <div class="section-head" id="pipeline"><h2>The pipeline behind this site</h2></div>
  <p class="lede">
    The <a href="/stats/">stats page</a> is fed by Home Assistant itself. No spreadsheets, and no
    cloud service sitting in the middle:
  </p>
  <ol style="max-width:40rem">
    <li><strong>Six daily utility_meter helpers</strong> wrap the Sigenergy cumulative energy
    sensors. When they reset at midnight, yesterday's total survives in the
    <code>last_period</code> attribute, which is the trick that makes a 00:15 read reliable.</li>
    <li><strong>Yesterday's costs</strong> come from Predbat's own tracking sensors
    (<code>cost_yesterday</code> and its no-solar comparison), which are ready right after
    midnight. The Octopus cost sensors lag a day behind, so they're no good here.</li>
    <li><strong>At 00:15</strong> an automation builds one JSON file for yesterday and pushes it to
    this site's GitHub repo with a single <code>rest_command</code> against the GitHub Contents
    API. One file per day, retried five times with backoff if the network blips.</li>
    <li><strong>GitHub Actions</strong> rebuilds the site and rsyncs it to the server. The commit
    history doubles as an audit log of every day's data.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The full pipeline YAML is on the <a href="/configs/">configs page</a> if you want it for your
  own install.</p>

  <div class="section-head"><h2>Maintenance reality</h2></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Cadence</th><th>Task</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Weekly</td><td>Check HA is up (an uptime monitor pings it anyway)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Monthly</td><td>HACS updates, and a sanity check of earnings against the Octopus bill</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Quarterly</td><td>Compare flat 12p export earnings with what Agile Outgoing would have paid. Switching is free</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Annually</td><td>Full backup, battery cycle count, panel inspection</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</Base>
